What each failure establishes
1. completionAndCancellationHaveOneWinner(cancelFirst:false), test lines 300–325: its two-second absolute request deadline includes startup, scripted completion, Foundation invalidation, the serial queue tail, and caller resumption. The generic "JPIP operation deadline elapsed" can arise at JPIPHTTPSRequest.read line 234 before session startup or at line 253 after the completed result is taken. The log cannot select one. Scripted completion sets taskCompleted, preventing a later watchdog action from replacing that result; the final wall-clock recheck can still reject it. A timeout here is compatible with correct wall-clock semantics and is not proof of two winners or lost completion.
2. completedRequestEventuallyReleasesItsDelegateOwnership, test lines 328–352: its operation has a two-second deadline before it reaches the separate deinitialisation observation. The direct request's "JPIP HTTPS operation deadline elapsed" identifies the request watchdog's latched error. It does not identify whether startup, protocol delivery, or delegate handling was delayed. This failure occurs before the test's deinitialisation assertion and is not a demonstrated retained-delegate leak.
3. sharedHTTPSPathPreservesRequestHeadersBodyAndNegotiatedChannel, lines 116–140: its deadline is constructed before the actor send. The same HTTPS deadline text can be generated either by the request watchdog or by JPIPTransport.sendViaHTTPS's catch remapping at lines 581–582 when the current clock is already past the deadline. The raw error therefore does not prove a watchdog firing or channel/header corruption.
4. sharedWatchdogInterruptsAnAlreadyActiveTrickle, lines 175–214: this read deliberately has deadline:nil. It failed the separate two-second readiness gate before arming its 120ms watchdog. The fixture reported 101 submitted chunks and a started protocol, while the accumulator reported zero bytes. The fixture increments submitted count before URLProtocolClient.didLoad, so this is not a receipt count. JPIPHTTPSRequest.receivedBodyBytes also returns zero after didComplete clears the buffer. Without callback/outcome events, zero cannot uniquely mean that no body callback ever arrived. The earlier standalone Content-Type experiment and focused10 success remain valid but do not explain this full-run observation.

Source facts relevant to scheduling
JPIPHTTPSRequest.read directly invokes synchronous startup inside withCheckedContinuation (lines 243–247). Startup constructs an OperationQueue and URLSession, creates a suspended dataTask under its state lock, then resumes it (lines 266–291). The delegate queue is serial; it has no explicitly assigned qualityOfService or underlyingQueue. Completion awaits both task completion and invalidation, then adds a serial-queue tail operation that finishes the broadcast and resumes the Swift continuation (lines 357–408). These are several independently schedulable phases. Their actual full10 durations are not measured. No source-level evidence justifies claiming a specific Foundation internal queue, MIME mechanism, cooperative-executor deadlock, or synchronous URLSession startup stall caused this failure.

The HTTPS fixture's serial dispatch queue, registry Mutex, and per-scenario Mutex contain no explicit wait for Swift task progress. Its short callbacks release locks before calling URLProtocolClient. No confirmed lock cycle was found in this bounded inspection. Synchronous URLSession creation/dataTask creation still require phase timing before their latency can be characterised.

